        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="hnpdLIrYYvueMlUQwmrZ-468" parent="hnpdLIrYYvueMlUQwmrZ-466" style="shape=partialRectangle;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;connectable=0;strokeColor=inherit;overflow=hidden;fillColor=light-dark(#FFFFFF,#FFFFFF);top=0;left=0;bottom=0;right=0;pointerEvents=1;fontColor=light-dark(#000000,#000000);fontSize=24;align=left;" value="&lt;blockquote style=&quot;margin: 0px 0px 0px 40px; border-width: medium; border-style: none; border-color: currentcolor; border-image: none; padding: 0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;[1] title/technology mismatch&lt;/strong&gt;: The abstract text I retrieved for this paper describes capacitors implemented &quot;in the BEoL of XFAB&#39;s XT018 technology,&quot; which sounds like the BEOL-integrated variant, not the CUB/MOL variant, yet the title matches the paper I&#39;d been citing as the CUB origin paper. This may mean [1] and [2] describe different integration approaches despite shared authorship, or that Okuno&#39;s group explored both. Verify directly against the full text before citing [1] specifically for the CUB claim, cite [2] for CUB, since that abstract explicitly and unambiguously describes the CUB structure and its &amp;gt;500°C anneal benefit.&lt;/blockquote&gt;" vertex="1">
